WEBVTT

00:00:01.651 --> 00:00:12.197
Penguin Readers Level 1: The Missing Coins by John Escott.

00:00:12.257 --> 00:00:18.250
Bath is a very old city in England.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:21.022
It has a beautiful abbey.

00:00:21.082 --> 00:00:25.554
A lot of people visit the city every year.

00:00:25.614 --> 00:00:31.986
And in the summer, there are always a lot of tourists in the streets.

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:36.927
Pete and Carla are students in Bath.

00:00:36.987 --> 00:00:39.308
They like the city.

00:00:39.368 --> 00:00:43.859
Pete likes looking at the beautiful old buildings.

00:00:43.919 --> 00:00:48.190
Carla likes walking in the little streets.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:53.682
She often finds pretty little shops there and goes into them.

00:00:53.742 --> 00:01:00.598
Or she sits with Pete in a cafe and watches the tourists.

00:01:00.658 --> 00:01:06.646
It's a Saturday in summer and a hot afternoon.

00:01:06.706 --> 00:01:10.678
There are a lot of tourists in the city today.

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:15.771
People from many countries are going into the Abbey.

00:01:15.831 --> 00:01:21.114
“Let’s find a quiet street away from the tourists,”

00:01:21.174 --> 00:01:22.775
Carla says.

00:01:22.835 --> 00:01:24.376
“Okay,”

00:01:24.436 --> 00:01:25.987
he says.

00:01:26.047 --> 00:01:28.868
He likes being with Carla.

00:01:28.928 --> 00:01:32.402
They walk away from the Abbey.

00:01:32.462 --> 00:01:38.298
After some time, they are in a little street behind the abbey.

00:01:38.358 --> 00:01:42.022
“This is pretty,” Carla says.

00:01:42.082 --> 00:01:46.736
There are only two or three tourists in this street.

00:01:46.795 --> 00:01:49.678
The shops have small windows.

00:01:49.738 --> 00:01:53.642
Some houses have pretty little gardens.

00:01:53.702 --> 00:01:59.674
It's quiet here, but Pete and Carla can hear music.

00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:02.534
“Look,” Pete says.

00:02:02.594 --> 00:02:05.586
That man is playing a flute.

00:02:05.646 --> 00:02:08.216
Let's go and listen.

00:02:08.276 --> 00:02:12.829
They walk across the street and watch the man.

00:02:12.888 --> 00:02:16.140
Four or five people are listening.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.671
“Why is he playing here?”

00:02:18.731 --> 00:02:20.302
Carla asks.

00:02:20.362 --> 00:02:23.864
There aren't many tourists in this street.

00:02:23.924 --> 00:02:26.624
“No,” Pete says.

00:02:26.684 --> 00:02:31.327
And there are only three or four coins in his hat.

00:02:31.387 --> 00:02:35.709
Pete and Carla listen to the flute player.

00:02:35.769 --> 00:02:39.050
The tune is Greensleeves.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:42.822
“I know that tune,” Carla says.

00:02:42.882 --> 00:02:46.363
And she puts a coin in the man's hat.

00:02:46.423 --> 00:02:48.974
Pete takes her hand.

00:02:49.034 --> 00:02:52.955
“Let’s go, Carla,” he says to her.

00:02:53.015 --> 00:02:56.456
Let's see the shops in the next street.

00:02:56.516 --> 00:03:01.596
“Okay,” Carla says. They walk away.

00:03:01.656 --> 00:03:08.830
The flute player watches them, but his tune, Greensleeves, doesn't stop.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:16.718
“He isn’t a very good flute player,” he says, “but it’s a good tune.”

00:03:16.778 --> 00:03:21.950
“There were only four coins in his hat,” Carla says.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:26.590
“That’s because he isn’t very good,” Pete says.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:29.854
And he's playing in the wrong street.

00:03:29.914 --> 00:03:33.386
The tourists are near the Abbey.

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:37.059
They walk to the next street.

00:03:37.119 --> 00:03:40.772
They find a little shop there.

00:03:40.831 --> 00:03:45.376
There are some old coins and stamps in the shop window.

00:03:45.436 --> 00:03:48.528
They come from many countries.

00:03:48.587 --> 00:03:51.924
“Let’s go in here,” Carla says.

00:03:51.984 --> 00:03:54.751
It's an interesting little shop.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.962
“Okay,” Pete says.

00:03:58.022 --> 00:04:00.904
The shop is small.

00:04:00.963 --> 00:04:06.667
Pete and Carla go in, and an old man watches them.

00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:09.898
He is the shopkeeper.

00:04:09.958 --> 00:04:13.942
“Good afternoon,” he says.

00:04:14.002 --> 00:04:15.650
“Good afternoon,”

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:22.573
Carla says. “Hi,” Pete says. “What can I do for you?”

00:04:22.632 --> 00:04:27.166
the shopkeeper says. “Can we look at the stamps?”

00:04:27.226 --> 00:04:31.980
Pete asks. The shopkeeper looks at them.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.090
“Okay,” he says.

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:38.478
“Do you collect stamps?”

00:04:38.538 --> 00:04:44.152
“No, but I’m going to start collecting them,” Pete says.

00:04:44.212 --> 00:04:46.814
They're interesting.

00:04:46.874 --> 00:04:51.657
“Yes, they are,” the shopkeeper says.

00:04:51.716 --> 00:04:58.220
Pete looks at some stamps from Canada and some stamps from South America.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:02.680
“These are beautiful stamps,” he says.

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:07.750
The shopkeeper stands near Pete and watches him.

00:05:07.809 --> 00:05:11.542
“He isn’t happy,” Carla thinks.

00:05:11.602 --> 00:05:13.072
Why?

00:05:13.132 --> 00:05:16.654
We aren't going to steal stamps.

00:05:16.714 --> 00:05:20.117
She looks at some coins.

00:05:20.176 --> 00:05:22.968
Are these coins very old?

00:05:23.028 --> 00:05:25.560
Carla asks the old man.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.573
“They’re 300 years old,” says the shopkeeper.

00:05:30.633 --> 00:05:33.074
They're very valuable.

00:05:33.134 --> 00:05:37.387
Every coin in my shop is valuable.

00:05:37.447 --> 00:05:41.019
A girl is working in the shop too.

00:05:41.079 --> 00:05:43.490
She looks at Carla.

00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:49.143
Carla smiles, but the girl doesn't smile at her.

00:05:49.203 --> 00:05:51.804
“She isn’t very friendly,”

00:05:51.864 --> 00:05:53.866
Carla thinks.

00:05:53.926 --> 00:05:59.368
Suddenly they hear a telephone in the room behind the shop.

00:05:59.427 --> 00:06:03.030
The shopkeeper doesn't answer it for a minute.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:07.613
Then he looks at the girl and she looks at him.

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:12.816
He doesn't speak, but Carla understands.

00:06:12.876 --> 00:06:20.733
The shopkeeper doesn’t speak, but he is saying to the girl, “Watch these students.”

00:06:20.793 --> 00:06:28.658
“Excuse me,” he says to Carla, and he goes into the back room.

00:06:28.718 --> 00:06:32.620
Pete is looking at the stamps.

00:06:32.679 --> 00:06:36.882
“Come and see these, Carla,” he says.

00:06:36.942 --> 00:06:39.764
They're from South America.

00:06:39.824 --> 00:06:43.187
I want to start collecting them.

00:06:43.247 --> 00:06:47.900
But Carla doesn't like the people in this shop.

00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:53.894
They aren't very friendly, and she wants to look at the city.

00:06:53.954 --> 00:06:58.007
“They’re very expensive,” she says.

00:06:58.067 --> 00:07:00.467
Let's go, Pete.

00:07:00.527 --> 00:07:05.481
The girl watches them and then she smiles.

00:07:05.541 --> 00:07:09.726
“She’s happy because we’re going,” Carla thinks.

00:07:09.785 --> 00:07:10.836
But why?

00:07:10.857 --> 00:07:17.324
Pete and Carla go back into the big streets with the tourists.

00:07:17.383 --> 00:07:22.077
Carla is happy now because they are not in the shop.

00:07:22.137 --> 00:07:27.358
But the sun is hot and Pete wants a drink.

00:07:27.418 --> 00:07:32.201
“Let’s go to a cafe near the Abbey,” he says.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:38.003
He wanted the stamps from South America, but he doesn't say this.

00:07:38.063 --> 00:07:41.625
There are tables outside the cafe.

00:07:41.685 --> 00:07:44.536
Pete and Carla sit down.

00:07:44.596 --> 00:07:48.248
There are a lot of people in the cafe.

00:07:48.308 --> 00:07:51.460
“Can we go into the Abbey next?”

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.360
Carla asks.

00:07:53.420 --> 00:08:00.686
“He wants the stamps from South America,” she thinks, “but they’re very expensive.”

00:08:00.746 --> 00:08:05.200
“I want to drink first,” Pete says.

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:09.844
“Okay,” Carla says, and she smiles at him.

00:08:09.904 --> 00:08:13.197
Pete smiles at her too.

00:08:13.257 --> 00:08:19.678
After a minute or two, they hear, “Hey, there you are!”

00:08:19.737 --> 00:08:22.336
Pete and Carla look up.

00:08:22.396 --> 00:08:25.342
It's the old man in the shop.

00:08:25.402 --> 00:08:27.386
“What does he want?”

00:08:27.446 --> 00:08:28.858
Pete says.

00:08:28.918 --> 00:08:31.693
“I don’t know,” Carla says.

00:08:31.753 --> 00:08:33.456
But he's angry.

00:08:33.516 --> 00:08:35.424
What's wrong?

00:08:35.484 --> 00:08:38.814
The shopkeeper comes to the cafe.

00:08:38.874 --> 00:08:41.285
He is very angry.

00:08:41.346 --> 00:08:43.828
“Where are my coins?”

00:08:43.888 --> 00:08:45.089
He says.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:47.942
“Your coins?” Carla says.

00:08:48.002 --> 00:08:49.444
I don't understand.

00:08:49.464 --> 00:08:54.038
“They’re missing,” the shopkeeper says.

00:08:54.098 --> 00:08:56.704
“Did you steal them?”

00:08:56.764 --> 00:09:00.100
Carla and Pete are surprised.

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:04.764
The people at the tables near them are surprised too.

00:09:04.824 --> 00:09:08.887
They watch the two friends and the old man.

00:09:08.946 --> 00:09:11.378
“Steal your coins?”

00:09:11.438 --> 00:09:13.950
“No,” Carla says.

00:09:14.010 --> 00:09:16.964
Her face is hot and red.

00:09:17.024 --> 00:09:22.218
“We didn’t steal any coins or stamps,” Pete says.

00:09:22.278 --> 00:09:24.220
What are you saying?

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:26.967
He is angry now.

00:09:27.027 --> 00:09:31.766
“You’ve got my coins,” the shopkeeper says.

00:09:31.826 --> 00:09:37.118
The coins are missing and this isn't the first time.

00:09:37.178 --> 00:09:40.230
Some coins were missing last week.

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:44.452
Some students had been there too, I think.

00:09:44.512 --> 00:09:48.084
“Did you see the students with the coins?”

00:09:48.144 --> 00:09:49.716
Pete says.

00:09:49.776 --> 00:09:53.254
The old man doesn't answer.

00:09:53.314 --> 00:09:57.834
A policeman walks down the street to the cafe.

00:09:57.894 --> 00:10:01.918
He watches Carla and Pete and the shopkeeper.

00:10:01.978 --> 00:10:06.451
Then he says, “What’s wrong?”

00:10:06.511 --> 00:10:12.456
“These two students came into my shop,” the shopkeeper says.

00:10:12.516 --> 00:10:16.958
I went to the back room and answered the telephone.

00:10:17.018 --> 00:10:20.401
Then I went back into the shop.

00:10:20.461 --> 00:10:23.240
Some valuable coins were missing.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:27.379
And the two students were not in the shop.

00:10:27.439 --> 00:10:31.650
The policeman looks at Pete and Carla.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:33.841
“What do you say?”

00:10:33.901 --> 00:10:35.482
He asks.

00:10:35.541 --> 00:10:38.743
“Yes, we went into the shop,”

00:10:38.802 --> 00:10:40.053
Carla says.

00:10:40.113 --> 00:10:43.174
We wanted to look at the stamps.

00:10:43.234 --> 00:10:47.635
“But we didn't steal them,” Pete says.

00:10:47.695 --> 00:10:51.149
And we didn't take the coins.

00:10:51.209 --> 00:10:54.744
“Were there many people in the shop?”

00:10:54.804 --> 00:10:57.135
The policeman asks.

00:10:57.195 --> 00:11:00.197
“No,” the shopkeeper says.

00:11:00.257 --> 00:11:04.370
Only these two students and Tracy.

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.951
Tracy works in my shop.

00:11:07.011 --> 00:11:09.952
She doesn't steal coins.

00:11:10.012 --> 00:11:12.254
“How do you know?”

00:11:12.314 --> 00:11:14.694
asks the policeman.

00:11:14.754 --> 00:11:24.108
“I looked in her bag and pockets today,” the shopkeeper says, “and I looked in them last week.”

00:11:24.168 --> 00:11:28.322
“You can look in my bag,” Carla says.

00:11:28.382 --> 00:11:33.366
“And you can look in my pockets,” Pete says.

00:11:33.426 --> 00:11:36.448
The shopkeeper is surprised.

00:11:36.508 --> 00:11:37.928
Can I?

00:11:37.988 --> 00:11:39.400
He says.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:41.742
“Please look.”

00:11:41.802 --> 00:11:45.358
“Please look now,” Carla says.

00:11:45.418 --> 00:11:48.344
We didn't steal any coins.

00:11:48.404 --> 00:11:51.156
She gives him her bag.

00:11:51.216 --> 00:11:57.090
The shopkeeper looks in Carla's bag and in their pockets.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:12:00.812
The people at the tables near them watch him.

00:12:00.873 --> 00:12:04.776
Is he going to find the coins, they think?

00:12:04.836 --> 00:12:09.186
But he finds no coins or stamps.

00:12:09.246 --> 00:12:14.224
“We didn’t steal the coins,” Pete says.

00:12:14.284 --> 00:12:19.056
And now you know that.

00:12:19.116 --> 00:12:22.568
“I’m sorry,” the shopkeeper says.

00:12:22.628 --> 00:12:27.410
I was wrong, but I don't understand it.

00:12:27.470 --> 00:12:30.492
Where are the coins?

00:12:30.552 --> 00:12:34.853
Then the old man goes back to his shop.

00:12:34.913 --> 00:12:40.480
“He isn’t very happy,” Pete says to the policeman.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:45.758
“Coins are very small,” the policeman says.

00:12:45.818 --> 00:12:50.962
Perhaps they're in the shop, but he can't see them.

00:12:51.022 --> 00:12:55.124
Some young people start to play music.

00:12:55.184 --> 00:12:57.956
A girl plays a flute.

00:12:58.016 --> 00:13:01.259
“We didn't steal any coins,”

00:13:01.319 --> 00:13:03.760
Carla says to the policeman.

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:07.666
“I know,” the policeman says.

00:13:07.726 --> 00:13:11.008
Carla listens to the music.

00:13:11.068 --> 00:13:13.760
It is Greensleeves.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.620
Where did I hear that?

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.171
She thinks.

00:13:18.231 --> 00:13:24.222
And then she remembers the flute player near the coin and stamp shop.

00:13:24.282 --> 00:13:28.324
“He played that tune,” she remembers.

00:13:28.384 --> 00:13:31.834
Pete is watching her face.

00:13:31.894 --> 00:13:33.555
What is it?

00:13:33.615 --> 00:13:35.055
He asks her.

00:13:35.116 --> 00:13:37.109
What's wrong?

00:13:37.169 --> 00:13:42.966
“I know now,” she says, “the missing coins.”

00:13:43.026 --> 00:13:45.388
I know the answer.

00:13:45.448 --> 00:13:46.860
Do you?

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:48.260
Pete says.

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:50.042
What is it?

00:13:50.102 --> 00:13:54.736
“Come with me,” she says to Pete and the policeman.

00:13:54.796 --> 00:13:58.699
We're going to find the man with the flute.

00:13:58.759 --> 00:14:00.860
Which man?

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:03.100
“Which man?”

00:14:03.160 --> 00:14:08.992
“He plays in the street near the old man’s shop,” Pete says.

00:14:09.052 --> 00:14:15.996
Then Carla, Pete, and the policeman run to the stamp and coin shop.

00:14:16.056 --> 00:14:19.988
“Hey, you!” Carla says.

00:14:20.048 --> 00:14:26.382
The flute player is going to walk away, but the policeman stops him.

00:14:26.442 --> 00:14:29.354
“Wait,” the policeman says.

00:14:29.414 --> 00:14:32.157
We want to speak to you.

00:14:32.217 --> 00:14:35.370
The man wants to run now.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:39.662
“I’m going home,” he says.

00:14:39.722 --> 00:14:43.754
“No, wait,” the policeman says.

00:14:43.814 --> 00:14:46.506
He looks at Carla.

00:14:46.566 --> 00:14:51.588
“You have the coins,” Carla says to the flute player.

00:14:51.648 --> 00:14:54.480
“No,” the man says.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:59.973
He starts to run away, but the policeman stops him again.

00:15:00.033 --> 00:15:02.118
How did he do it?

00:15:02.178 --> 00:15:04.413
Pete asks Carla.

00:15:04.473 --> 00:15:08.814
“It was the girl in the shop,” Carla says.

00:15:08.874 --> 00:15:12.865
We looked at some stamps, remember?

00:15:12.925 --> 00:15:18.446
And the shopkeeper was on the telephone in the room at the back.

00:15:18.506 --> 00:15:23.047
“But how did the flute player get the coins?”

00:15:23.107 --> 00:15:24.997
The policeman asks.

00:15:25.057 --> 00:15:27.668
“Yes,” Pete says.

00:15:27.728 --> 00:15:30.458
I don't understand.

00:15:30.517 --> 00:15:34.808
“He was outside the window,” Carla says.

00:15:34.868 --> 00:15:39.080
The girl went to the window with the coins.

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:44.242
“But there were people in the street,” he says.

00:15:44.302 --> 00:15:49.164
“Yes, perhaps people watched her,” Carla says.

00:15:49.224 --> 00:15:54.256
But the coins went into the flute player's hat.

00:15:54.316 --> 00:16:00.706
The coins were valuable, but the people in the street didn't know that.

00:16:00.766 --> 00:16:07.078
“The girl and the flute player were very clever,” Pete says.

00:16:07.138 --> 00:16:14.023
“But now they’re coming with me to the police station,” the policeman says.

00:16:14.083 --> 00:16:18.196
He takes the coins from the flute player.

00:16:18.256 --> 00:16:22.490
After some time, a police car arrives.

00:16:22.550 --> 00:16:27.826
It takes the girl and the flute player to the police station.

00:16:27.887 --> 00:16:32.592
“Thank you,” the shopkeeper says to Carla.

00:16:32.652 --> 00:16:35.473
The coins are valuable.

00:16:35.533 --> 00:16:38.244
I want to say thank you.

00:16:38.304 --> 00:16:41.006
What can I give you?

00:16:41.066 --> 00:16:43.598
Carla smiles.

00:16:43.658 --> 00:16:48.550
“Give Pete the stamps from South America,” she says.

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:52.191
Then he can start his stamp collecting today.

00:16:52.211 --> 00:16:56.360
He gives her a big smile.

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:58.294
“Thanks, Carla.”

00:16:58.354 --> 00:17:00.929
He says, “I love you.”